!id: zlAnpCeH3o
!front: State the algebraic definition of the **Cross Product**.
!back:
Consider two vectors \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) in the 3-dimensional space, \( \mathbf{a} = \langle a_1, a_2, a_3 \rangle \), \( \mathbf{b} = \langle b_1, b_2, b_3 \rangle \).

The **Cross Product** \( \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} \) is the determinant

\[
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} =
\begin{vmatrix}
  \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
  a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\
  b_1 & b_2 & b_3
\end{vmatrix}
\]

which expands to

\[
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} =
(a_2 b_3 - a_3 b_2) \mathbf{i} -
(a_1 b_3 - a_3 b_1) \mathbf{j} +
(a_1 b_2 - a_2 b_1) \mathbf{k}
\]
~~~

!id: ab8XVzqdg3
!front: State the geometric definition of the **Cross Product**.
!back:
Consider two vectors \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) in the 3-dimensional space, \( \mathbf{a} = \langle a_1, a_2, a_3 \rangle \), \( \mathbf{b} = \langle b_1, b_2, b_3 \rangle \).

The **Cross Product** \( \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} \) is a vector that:

- is perpendicular to both \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) (so orthogonal to the plane containing them),
- has magnitude equal to the area of the parallelogram spanned by \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \):
  \[ \|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}\| = \| \mathbf{a} \| \| \mathbf{b} \| \sin \theta \]
  where \( \theta \) is the angle between \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \), and
- has direction given by the right-hand rule.

![The Cross Product a x b, perpendicular to the plane spanned by a and b, whose magnitude equals the area of the parallelogram they span](img/cross_product.svg)
~~~

!id: 4wCxwXidGy
!front: Why is \( \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \mathbf{0} \), for nonzero \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \), equivalent to \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) being parallel?
!back: From the geometric definition, \( \|\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b}\| = \|\mathbf{a}\|\|\mathbf{b}\|\sin\theta \). For nonzero \( \mathbf{a}, \mathbf{b} \), this vanishes exactly when \( \sin\theta = 0 \), i.e. \( \theta = 0 \) or \( \theta = \pi \) — the vectors point in the same or exactly opposite direction, which is precisely the condition for \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) to be parallel (one a scalar multiple of the other).
~~~

!id: V2TTEVlBd6
!front: Does the cross product satisfy the associative law \( (\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b})\times\mathbf{c} = \mathbf{a}\times(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}) \)? If not, construct a counterexample.
!back: No. Counterexample using standard basis vectors: \( (\mathbf{i}\times\mathbf{i})\times\mathbf{j} = \mathbf{0}\times\mathbf{j} = \mathbf{0} \), but \( \mathbf{i}\times(\mathbf{i}\times\mathbf{j}) = \mathbf{i}\times\mathbf{k} = -\mathbf{j} \). Since \( \mathbf{0} \ne -\mathbf{j} \), the cross product is not associative.
~~~

!id: gz0pCSyHkn
!front: Is the cross product commutative? If not, what law replaces commutativity?
!back:
No. The cross product is anti-commutative:

\[ \mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b} = -(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{a}). \]

Swapping the order keeps the same magnitude but reverses the direction, because the right-hand rule chooses the opposite normal vector.
~~~

!id: ZzQIcgdrs6
!front: How does the cross product distribute over vector addition and scalar multiplication?
!back:
For \( \mathbf{a},\mathbf{b},\mathbf{c}\in\mathbb{R}^3 \) and scalar \( \lambda \),

\[
(\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b})\times\mathbf{c}
=
\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{c}
+
\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c},
\]

\[
\mathbf{a}\times(\mathbf{b}+\mathbf{c})
=
\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b}
+
\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{c},
\]

and

\[
(\lambda\mathbf{a})\times\mathbf{b}
=
\lambda(\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b})
=
\mathbf{a}\times(\lambda\mathbf{b}).
\]

Together, these say the cross product is bilinear.
~~~

!id: CLI18uzGxy
!front: What are the cross products of the standard basis vectors \( \mathbf{i},\mathbf{j},\mathbf{k} \)?
!back:
In cyclic order,

\[
\mathbf{i}\times\mathbf{j}=\mathbf{k},
\qquad
\mathbf{j}\times\mathbf{k}=\mathbf{i},
\qquad
\mathbf{k}\times\mathbf{i}=\mathbf{j}.
\]

Reversing the order changes the sign:

\[
\mathbf{j}\times\mathbf{i}=-\mathbf{k},
\qquad
\mathbf{k}\times\mathbf{j}=-\mathbf{i},
\qquad
\mathbf{i}\times\mathbf{k}=-\mathbf{j}.
\]

Also, each basis vector crossed with itself is \( \mathbf{0} \).
~~~

!id: lzL3HuLWdp
!front: How does \( \|\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b}\| \) measure parallelogram and triangle area?
!back:
For \( \mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}\in\mathbb{R}^3 \),

\[ \|\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b}\| = \|\mathbf{a}\|\,\|\mathbf{b}\|\sin\theta, \]

where \( \theta \) is the angle between them. This is base times height, so it is the area of the parallelogram spanned by \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \).

The area of the triangle with sides \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) from a common tail is therefore

\[ \frac{1}{2}\|\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b}\|. \]
~~~

!id: c0SN7CZfc2
!front: How does the cross product give a normal vector to a plane?
!back:
If \( \mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^3 \) are nonparallel direction vectors lying in a plane, then

\[ \mathbf{n}=\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v} \]

is nonzero and perpendicular to both \( \mathbf{u} \) and \( \mathbf{v} \). Therefore \( \mathbf{n} \) is normal to the plane they span.

If the plane passes through a point \( \mathbf{p} \), its equation can be written as

\[ (\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{p})\cdot(\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v})=0. \]
!extra: The nonparallel hypothesis is necessary. If \( \mathbf{u} \) and \( \mathbf{v} \) are parallel, then \( \mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{0} \), which is not a normal vector.
~~~

!id: Q5oD411Oua
!front: What is the product rule for the cross product of differentiable vector-valued functions?
!back:
If \( \mathbf{r},\mathbf{s}:I\to\mathbb{R}^3 \) are differentiable at \( t \), then

\[
\frac{d}{dt}\bigl(\mathbf{r}(t)\times\mathbf{s}(t)\bigr)
=
\mathbf{r}'(t)\times\mathbf{s}(t)
+
\mathbf{r}(t)\times\mathbf{s}'(t).
\]

The order of the factors must be preserved, because the cross product is anti-commutative, not commutative.
~~~

!id: Aijs0Nbgsd
!front: What is the scalar triple product and what does it measure geometrically?
!back:
The scalar triple product is

\[ \mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}). \]

Geometrically, its absolute value is the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by \( \mathbf{a},\mathbf{b},\mathbf{c} \):

\[
\operatorname{Vol}
=
\left|\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})\right|.
\]

The sign records orientation: it is positive or negative depending on whether the ordered triple \( (\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b},\mathbf{c}) \) agrees with the chosen orientation of \( \mathbb{R}^3 \).

![The parallelepiped spanned by a, b, c: its base is the parallelogram spanned by b and c, and b x c is normal to that base, so the volume is the base area times the height of a along the b x c direction](img/scalar_triple_product.svg)
~~~
